.Dd July 31, 2026
.Dt LIBCHANNEL 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm libchannel
.Nd asynchronous userspace capability-channel messaging
.Sh LIBRARY
Capability channel library
.Pq libchannel, Fl lchannel
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In channel.h
.Ft int
.Fn channel_create "int fd" "const struct channel_options *options" "struct channel **channel"
.Ft void
.Fn channel_destroy "struct channel *channel"
.Ft void
.Fn channel_abandon "struct channel *channel"
.Ft int
.Fn channel_fd "const struct channel *channel"
.Ft int
.Fn channel_error "const struct channel *channel"
.Ft int
.Fn channel_wants_write "const struct channel *channel"
.Ft int
.Fo channel_send_request
.Fa "struct channel *channel"
.Fa "const struct channel_outgoing *message"
.Fa "channel_reply_handler handler"
.Fa "void *context"
.Fa "struct channel_request **request"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fn channel_send_event "struct channel *channel" "const struct channel_outgoing *message"
.Ft int
.Fn channel_set_request_handler "struct channel *channel" "channel_request_handler handler" "void *context"
.Ft int
.Fn channel_set_event_handler "struct channel *channel" "channel_event_handler handler" "void *context"
.Ft int
.Fn channel_dispatch "struct channel *channel"
.Ft int
.Fn channel_flush "struct channel *channel"
.Ft int
.Fn channel_send_reply "struct channel_message *request" "const struct channel_outgoing *message"
.Ft int
.Fn channel_request_cancel "struct channel_request *request"
.Ft int
.Fn channel_request_status "const struct channel_request *request"
.Ft void
.Fn channel_request_release "struct channel_request *request"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
library provides a nonblocking, event-loop-native abstraction over
.Dv MAC_CAPABILITY_SENDMSG
and
.Dv MAC_CAPABILITY_RECVMSG .
It does not use or expose
.Dv MAC_CAPABILITY_CALL .
.Pp
A channel owns the descriptor passed successfully to
.Fn channel_create .
The descriptor is made nonblocking and is closed by
.Fn channel_destroy .
The caller registers
.Fn channel_fd
with kqueue for read readiness, and for write readiness while messages are
queued.
.Fn channel_dispatch
drains received messages and
.Fn channel_flush
drains the bounded outgoing queue.
.Fn channel_wants_write
reports whether write readiness should be enabled.
.Pp
Requests have fresh nonzero correlation tokens.
Events have token zero.
Replies are matched to outstanding requests before provider-side request
classification.
Unknown or stale replies are discarded and all of their descriptors are
closed.
Peer death completes every outstanding request with an error.
.Pp
Roles are deliberately directional.
A client may originate requests and a provider receives nonzero-token
requests and responds.
Either role may send events.
A provider cannot originate a request because an unknown late reply would
otherwise be indistinguishable from a new request without adding a redundant
transport envelope.
.Pp
Payloads and descriptors passed for sending remain caller-owned.
When backpressure requires queuing,
.Nm
copies the payload and duplicates the descriptors.
Ownership of a received message is transferred to the installed handler.
The handler may retain a provider request and respond later.
Received messages own their installed descriptors.
.Fn channel_message_take_fd
transfers one descriptor to the caller; all untaken descriptors are closed
when the message is freed.
Every delivered message must eventually be passed to
.Fn channel_message_free .
.Pp
Handlers execute synchronously from
.Fn channel_dispatch .
The API is intended for one dispatch context and does not create threads.
A handler may request destruction; actual storage reclamation is deferred
until dispatch unwinds.
.Pp
.Fn channel_abandon
is the post-fork escape hatch.
It releases inherited heap bookkeeping without invoking callbacks or closing
numeric descriptor slots which may already have been closed and reused in the
child.
It must not be used for ordinary shutdown.
.Pp
Cancellation is local only.
A late remote response is treated as stale and discarded.
Remote cancellation, application schemas, operation codes, delivery
guarantees, and descriptor-slot meanings belong to the service protocol
above this library.
.Sh CHANNEL CREATION
The caller initializes
.Vt struct channel_options
with
.Dv CHANNEL_OPTIONS_INITIALIZER .
The
.Va size
field must be at least the structure size.
Larger structures are accepted for forward-compatible extension.
The role is one of:
.Bl -tag -width CHANNEL_ROLE_PROVIDER
.It Dv CHANNEL_ROLE_CLIENT
May originate requests.
An unmatched nonzero token is a stale reply and is discarded.
.It Dv CHANNEL_ROLE_PROVIDER
May receive nonzero-token requests and respond to them.
It cannot originate requests.
.El
.Pp
The remaining fields bound pending requests and locally queued messages,
payload bytes, and descriptors.
All limits must be selected before untrusted traffic is accepted.
A successful
.Fn channel_create
consumes
.Fa fd .
The channel uses a private close-on-fork and close-on-exec duplicate, so its
descriptor number is not required to equal
.Fa fd ;
call
.Fn channel_fd
for event-loop registration.
On failure the caller retains it.
.Pp
Every send accepts a versioned
.Vt struct channel_outgoing .
Its
.Va data
and
.Va fds
members remain borrowed for the duration of the call.
The payload must be nonempty, and attachment slots are interpreted only by
the service protocol above
.Nm .
The
.Va size
field may be larger than the current structure size.
.Sh EVENT LOOP
.Fn channel_fd
returns the borrowed kernel descriptor.
Register it for
.Dv EVFILT_READ .
After a send operation, call
.Fn channel_wants_write ;
enable
.Dv EVFILT_WRITE
only while it returns one.
.Fn channel_dispatch
processes at most 64 messages per call so one busy channel cannot monopolize
an event loop.
It returns the number processed, including messages discarded by policy.
.Fn channel_flush
attempts to drain the outgoing queue and treats continued
.Er EAGAIN
as successful retained backpressure.
.Pp
The library has no internal lock.
Creation, dispatch, sending, cancellation, and destruction of one channel must
be serialized by its owner.
Callbacks run synchronously in the thread calling
.Fn channel_dispatch .
They may retain messages, enqueue responses or events, cancel other requests,
or call
.Fn channel_destroy .
They must not recursively dispatch the same channel.
.Sh REQUESTS AND RESPONSES
.Fn channel_send_request
registers
.Fa handler
before attempting the send, generates a fresh nonzero token, and returns a
request handle through
.Fa request .
The payload must be nonempty.
The input payload and descriptor array remain caller-owned.
The handler is invoked exactly once with either a reply and error zero, or a
null reply and a terminal or cancellation error.
The handler owns a nonnull reply and must free it.
.Pp
.Fn channel_send_reply
uses the token and channel retained by an incoming provider request.
It rejects token-zero messages, client-side replies, and duplicate responses.
A provider may keep the request after its handler returns and respond
asynchronously.
It must eventually free the request even if no response is sent.
.Pp
.Fn channel_request_cancel
removes an unsent queued request when possible and completes its callback with
.Er ECANCELED .
Cancellation does not send a remote message.
A response already in flight is later recognized as stale.
.Fn channel_request_status
returns
.Er EINPROGRESS
while pending and otherwise the completion error, with zero denoting a reply.
.Fn channel_request_release
releases the caller's handle reference.
It may be called while the request is pending or by its callback; reclamation
is deferred until completion when necessary.
.Sh EVENTS
.Fn channel_send_event
sends a token-zero message.
Either role may send events.
.Fn channel_set_event_handler
installs the receiver.
When no event handler is installed, events are discarded and all attached
descriptors are closed.
.Fn channel_set_request_handler
installs the provider request handler.
When no request handler is installed, incoming requests are discarded.
.Sh MESSAGE OWNERSHIP
The following accessors inspect a received message:
.Bl -tag -width channel_message_fd_count
.It Fn channel_message_kind
Returns request, reply, or event classification.
.It Fn channel_message_data
Returns borrowed payload storage valid until the message is freed.
.It Fn channel_message_length
Returns the kernel-reported payload length.
.It Fn channel_message_token
Returns the channel token; events return zero.
.It Fn channel_message_sender
Returns borrowed authenticated badge, nonce, uid, gid, and prison metadata.
.It Fn channel_message_fd_count
Returns the exact attachment count.
.It Fn channel_message_borrow_fd
Returns a borrowed installed descriptor for one zero-based attachment slot.
.It Fn channel_message_take_fd
Transfers one installed descriptor to the caller and marks its slot empty.
.El
.Pp
.Fn channel_message_free
closes every untaken descriptor and releases the payload and retained channel
reference.
Attachment slots preserve sender array order; sender descriptor numbers are
never part of the message.
.Sh SHUTDOWN AND FORK
.Fn channel_destroy
closes the descriptor, drops queued messages and descriptors, and completes
pending requests with
.Er ECANCELED .
When called by a callback, final reclamation waits until dispatch unwinds.
.Pp
.Fn channel_abandon
is only for the child side of
.Xr fork 2
when callbacks and locks may belong to vanished threads.
Channel descriptors and hidden queued attachment duplicates are locked
close-on-fork and close-on-exec.
Every channel and received attachment is process-bound.
An inherited operation fails with
.Er ECHILD .
.Fn channel_abandon
then frees inherited heap bookkeeping without invoking callbacks or closing
numeric descriptor slots which may already have been closed and reused in the
child.
.Pp
.Fn channel_error
returns zero while healthy.
After peer death or another terminal transport failure it returns \-1 and
restores the stable terminal error in
.Va errno .
.Sh RETURN VALUES
Unless documented otherwise, functions returning
.Vt int
return zero on success and \-1 on failure.
.Fn channel_dispatch
returns a nonnegative message count on success.
.Fn channel_fd
returns a nonnegative borrowed descriptor on success.
.Fn channel_wants_write
returns zero or one.
.Fn channel_request_status
returns an errno value directly.
Message accessors return their documented neutral value for a null message.
.Sh ERRORS
Outgoing limits produce
.Er ENOBUFS .
Backpressure is retained internally and is not reported as failure.
Protocol-role and ownership violations produce
.Er EINVAL .
Invalid or already completed cancellation produces
.Er EALREADY .
Payloads larger than the kernel channel maximum are rejected with
.Er EINVAL .
The terminal channel error is available through
.Fn channel_error .
.Sh DTRACE PROVIDER
When built with DTrace support,
.Nm
publishes the
.Qq libchannel
USDT provider.
Its probes cover channel creation, immediate sends, local queueing, received
message classification, request completion, policy discards, and terminal
peer death.
Payload contents and descriptor numbers are never exposed by these probes.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr fork 2 ,
.Xr kqueue 2 ,
.Xr libservice 3 ,
.Xr capsicum 4
